WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
Is a trout closer to a shark, or to you?

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.960
Shark and trout look alike, while you look different.

3
00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:08.460
Outward looks can mislead.

4
00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:13.780
Phylogenetic trees test which species share a more recent ancestor.

5
00:00:14.933 --> 00:00:19.253
To build a tree, compare characters or gene sequences across species.

6
00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:24.133
First, add an outgroup, a lineage that branched off earlier.

7
00:00:25.813 --> 00:00:29.233
Character states matching it are ancestral, from an older ancestor.

8
00:00:30.633 --> 00:00:32.613
Old states cannot show who split last.

9
00:00:33.893 --> 00:00:37.293
Only a new, shared derived state marks closest relatives.

10
00:00:38.417 --> 00:00:42.177
Every branching point, or node, marks a common ancestor.

11
00:00:43.557 --> 00:00:45.177
Characters change along the branches.

12
00:00:46.817 --> 00:00:48.397
Trout is closer to you than to a shark.

13
00:00:49.697 --> 00:00:54.677
The ancestor trout shares with you is more recent; it descends from the ancestor

14
00:00:54.677 --> 00:00:55.897
shared with the shark.

15
00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:58.510
Now watch the tree rotate.

16
00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:03.970
Suppose we spin the trout and human node so trout sits beside shark.

17
00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:07.710
Does spinning that node make trout closer to the shark?

18
00:01:13.783 --> 00:01:20.983
No. Rotating a node changes only tip order, never which shared ancestor is more

19
00:01:20.983 --> 00:01:27.303
recent. Neither tip order nor the count of nodes between tips shows relatedness.

20
00:01:28.843 --> 00:01:31.843
Relatedness lives at the nodes; change lives on the branches.

21
00:01:31.843 --> 00:01:38.703
On scaled molecular trees, branch lengths show estimated sequence change, not years.

22
00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:42.680
Trees are hypotheses, and traps can mislead.

23
00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:47.900
Two lineages can gain a similar state separately, called convergence.

24
00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:50.680
An outgroup can change too.

25
00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:54.740
One gene's tree can differ from the species tree.

26
00:01:56.060 --> 00:02:01.540
So studies compare several outgroups, use many genes and characters, and report how

27
00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:03.440
firmly the data support each node.

28
00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.520
Using trees carries limits too.

29
00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:11.820
Turning sequence change into time needs fossils anchored at nodes.

30
00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:18.840
In 2012, Parham and colleagues argued a misplaced or misdated fossil carries error

31
00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:23.420
into the dates. Related species are not independent points.

32
00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:29.500
So phylogenetic regression accounts for shared ancestry when testing traits.

33
00:02:30.567 --> 00:02:33.227
With those guards, trees reshaped biology.

34
00:02:34.647 --> 00:02:39.227
In 1977, Woese and George Fox compared short RNA pieces.

35
00:02:40.647 --> 00:02:46.487
In 1990, Woese, Otto Kandler and Mark Wheelis proposed three domains: Bacteria,

36
00:02:46.907 --> 00:02:47.907
Archaea and Eukarya.

37
00:02:49.327 --> 00:02:54.587
Many-gene trees now nest eukaryotes inside archaea, closest to a group called Asgard

38
00:02:54.587 --> 00:02:56.947
archaea, though the branch is debated.

39
00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:01.340
Gene trees broke old rankings of animal bodies.

40
00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:09.900
In 1997, Anna Marie Aguinaldo, James Lake and colleagues compared 18S ribosomal RNA

41
00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:16.060
genes. They grouped nematodes with arthropods and other moulting animals, not

42
00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:17.060
annelids.

43
00:03:17.583 --> 00:03:19.243
Plant trees shifted too.

44
00:03:20.503 --> 00:03:26.423
In 2019, the One Thousand Plant Transcriptomes Initiative compared genes from more

45
00:03:26.423 --> 00:03:27.703
than 1,100 species.

46
00:03:29.263 --> 00:03:34.323
Its nuclear gene trees recovered living mosses, liverworts and hornworts as one

47
00:03:34.323 --> 00:03:39.963
branch. A living moss is a cousin of vascular plants, not their ancestor.

48
00:03:41.383 --> 00:03:44.763
Every tree maps descent from shared nodes, not outward looks.
